{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 40, :comments ["<b>פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר (fruit of a beautiful tree)</b> — whose taste of its tree and its fruit are the same, \"הָדָר\" (beautiful) in that it remains on its tree from year to year, and this is the etrog — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 23:40). But these are Scriptural supports (asmachtot) that our Sages made (Sifra ad loc.; Sukkah 35a) for their reception of the tradition. And R. Abraham Ibn Ezra wrote that they spoke the truth, for there is no tree-fruit more beautiful (hadar) than it, and they expounded in it \"הַדָּר בְּאִילָנוֹ\" (beautiful in its tree) as a Scriptural support. But the correct view in my eyes is that the tree called in Aramaic \"etrog\" is called by its name in the sacred language \"hadar,\" for the meaning of etrog is \"desirable thing\" (chemdah), as we translate (Genesis 2:9) \"נֶחְמָד לְמַרְאֶה\" — \"דִּמְרַגַּג לְמִחְזֵי,\" and (Deuteronomy 5:18) \"לֹא תַחְמֹד\" — \"לֹא תִרֹג,\" and we say (Shabbat 145b) מִכָּל שְׁפַר אֲרַג נִכְסִין (every beautiful thing he has woven of possessions). And the expressions chemdah (desire) and hadar (beauty) are equivalent in meaning. And the tree and the fruit are called by one name, for so is the custom with most fruits: fig, nut, pomegranate, olive, and others. So too here, the name of the tree and the fruit — both in Aramaic etrog and in the sacred language hadar. And the plain sense of Scripture will say that we are to take for ourselves fruit of the tree hadar, and we are to take fronds of date-palms and one branch from the tree of thick foliage and one branch from willows of the brook — and behold they are one of each kind, for \"fronds of date-palms\" is connected to \"and you shall take for yourselves,\" which is plural, for the halakhah follows Rabbi Akiva, who says (Sukkah 34b): just as there is one lulav and one etrog, so too one myrtle and one willow. And therefore Onkelos translated (Onkelos on Leviticus 23:40) all of them in plural language, connected to \"and you shall take for yourselves,\" which is plural. And regarding the reason for this commandment, they said (Ta'anit 2b) by way of Aggadah that these species come to appease concerning the water. And by way of truth, \"פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר\" is the fruit in which desire was most abundant and through which the first man sinned, as it is stated (Genesis 3:6): \"And the woman saw that the tree was good for food and that it was a delight to the eyes, and the tree was desirable to make wise, and she took of its fruit and ate\" — and behold the sin was through it alone, and we come to appease before Him with the rest of the species. And \"כַּפּוֹת תְּמָרִים\" is the central line, doubled and elevated above them all. \"וַעֲנַף עֵץ עָבֹת\" alludes to three sefirot in one bond, as it is stated: \"מִידֵי אֲבִיר יַעֲקֹב\" (Genesis 49:24). \"וְעַרְבֵי נָחַל\" — as it is stated: \"סֹלּוּ לָרֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת\" (Psalms 68:5), for the attribute of judgment is mixed (mit'arevet) with the attribute of mercy. From here you will understand and know that the etrog is not bound together with them in the bundle, yet it is indispensable with them, for it corresponds to the Atzeret, which is a festival in its own right and the completion of the first day — and behold they are one in potentiality and not in actuality, and I have already explained this reason (Ramban on Leviticus 23:36). Now behold, the reason for the entire portion: you shall celebrate the great festival of HaShem seven days — which are the seven days of Creation — and you shall support them with the eighth day of Atzeret, as it is stated: \"לַמְנַצֵּחַ עַל הַשְּׁמִינִית\" (Psalms 6:1). And during those seven days you shall take among them fruit of the tree hadar and the lulav bound in a bundle — and therefore the etrog was mentioned first. But on the eighth day it is not needed, for it itself is hadar. And this is the reason for \"וְחַגֹּתֶם אֹתוֹ חַג לַה' שִׁבְעַת יָמִים בַּשָּׁנָה\" — that you shall celebrate the seven days together with the year, the idea being encircling and surrounding, from the language of \"וְחוּג שָׁמַיִם\" (Job 22:14), \"וּבַמְּחוּגָה יְתָאֳרֵהוּ\" (Isaiah 44:13), and likewise \"הָמוֹן חוֹגֵג\" (Psalms 42:5). And our Sages have already alluded to this secret. They said in Vayikra Rabbah (30:9): \"פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר\" — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is stated: \"הוֹד וְהָדָר לְפָנָיו\" (Psalms 96:6). \"כַּפּוֹת תְּמָרִים\" — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is stated: \"צַדִּיק כַּתָּמָר יִפְרָח\" (Psalms 92:13). \"וַעֲנַף עֵץ עָבֹת\" — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is stated: \"וְהוּא עֹמֵד בֵּין הַהֲדַסִּים אֲשֶׁר בַּמְּצֻלָה\" (Zechariah 1:8). \"וְעַרְבֵי נָחַל\" — this is the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is stated: \"סֹלּוּ לָרֹכֵב בָּעֲרָבוֹת\" (Psalms 68:5). And in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben HaKanah: What is \"פְּרִי עֵץ הָדָר\"? As we translate: פֵּירֵי אִילָנָא אֶתְרוֹגִין וְלוּלַבִּין. And what is \"הָדָר\"? That is, hadar of everything. And that is, the hadar of the Song of Songs, concerning which it is written: \"מִי זֹאת הַנִּשְׁקָפָה כְּמוֹ שָׁחַר\" and so forth (Song of Songs 6:10). And why is its name called \"הָדָר\"? Do not read hadar but hadar — this is the etrog, which is separate from the binding of the lulav, yet the commandment of lulav is fulfilled only with it, and it is bound together with all of them, for it is with each one individually and it is with all of them together. And what is the lulav? It corresponds to the spinal cord. And \"וַעֲנַף עֵץ עָבֹת\" — whose branches cover most of it, a parable to a man who shields his head with his arms: a branch on the left, thick foliage on the right, so the tree is in the middle. And why is \"עֵץ\" said of it? Because it is the root of the tree. And what is \"עַרְבֵי נָחַל\"? After the name of the place where they are fixed, whose name is nachal, as it is written: \"כָּל הַנְּחָלִים הֹלְכִים אֶל הַיָּם\" and so forth (Ecclesiastes 1:7). And what is the sea? You shall say: this is the etrog. And whence do we know that each and every measure of all these seven is called nachal? As it is stated: \"וּמִמַּתָּנָה נַחֲלִיאֵל\" (Numbers 21:19) — do not read Nachaliel but nachalei El, and so forth. And this midrash follows the opinion of three myrtles and two willows, one lulav and one etrog — and this is the halakhah according to all the Geonim and the early authorities."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The phrase 'דִּמְרַגַּג לְמִחְזֵי' is Onkelos's Aramaic translation of 'נֶחְמָד לְמַרְאֶה' (Genesis 2:9), retained in Aramaic script as Ramban quotes it to make his etymological argument. The citation מִכָּל שְׁפַר אֲרַג נִכְסִין (Shabbat 145b) is left in Aramaic as Ramban quotes it."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:59:25.858367543Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}